<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Namibia</h1><p class="st-meta">1990 CE – present · capital Windhoek</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A southern African republic that became independent from South Africa on 21 March 1990, after a long insurgency and a transition supervised by the UN.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/south-west-africa">South West Africa</a> was administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandate</a> from 1920. The UN renamed it Namibia in 1968, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a>'s insurgency against South African rule continued for years. South Africa accepted independence under the Tripartite Accord, and a UN transition group supervised elections in which SWAPO won 57% of the vote. Namibia became independent on 21 March 1990, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sam-nujoma">Sam Nujoma</a> as first president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The constitution adopted in February 1990 protected human rights, set up an independent judiciary and legislature and created an executive presidency. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walvis-bay">Walvis Bay</a> in 1994. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a> has won every election since independence, and the government has followed a policy of national reconciliation. In 1999 the government put down a secessionist attempt in the Caprivi Strip led by the Caprivi Liberation Army. The economy rests on agriculture, tourism and mining, and inequality and poverty are high. Nujoma was succeeded by Hifikepunye Pohamba in 2005.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Hage Geingob won the 2014 presidential election and was re-elected in December 2019. He died on 4 February 2024 and Nangolo Mbumba finished his term. Netumbo Nandi-Ndaitwah, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a>'s first female presidential candidate, won the 2024 elections with 57% of the vote and was sworn in on 21 March 2025.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/namibia.jpg" alt="Namibia" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: SVG by Vzb83~commonswiki et al. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Namibia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1990 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Windhoek</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sam Nujoma, Hifikepunye Pohamba, Hage Geingob, Nangolo Mbumba, Netumbo Nandi-Ndaitwah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity (mostly Lutheran), indigenous beliefs</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English (official), Oshiwambo, Afrikaans, German, Khoekhoegowab</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/namibia">Namibia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Namibia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=namibia" aria-label="Namibia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5137 3011 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Namibia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5457 3267L5457 3267"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5457 3267h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5457 3267h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5457 3267h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Windhoek. <a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=namibia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
